# Sizing profile for the PyG assembly leg (--mode pyg_only). Source it BEFORE the
# variables that identify your cluster:
#
#   . bin/profiles/pyg-assembly.env
#   . ~/my-cluster.env          # master URL, driver host, bucket paths -- NOT tracked
#   bin/submit_spark_job.sh --mode pyg_only ...
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT large-run.env: the two legs pull in opposite directions. Enrichment
# is executor-heavy -- hundreds of millions of triples joined and aggregated out on the
# cluster, nothing large on the driver. Assembly is the reverse. The executors only sort
# and join; the HeteroData itself is built in the driver's own memory, and it is big.
#
# WHAT THE DRIVER HAS TO HOLD, measured on the 2026-08 graph (10,348,460 nodes across
# 72 types, 76,764,031 edges):
#
#   node features   10,348,460 x 1024 x 4B  = 42.4 GiB   <- the dominant term
#   edge indices    76,764,031 x 2 x 8B     =  1.2 GiB
#   edge features   76,764,031 x 32 x 4B    =  9.8 GiB   (featurized types only)
#                                             --------
#                                              53.4 GiB  held at the same time, plus
#                                                        whatever torch.save copies
#
# Almost none of that is JVM heap. numpy owns it, outside -Xmx, so DRIVER_MEMORY does
# not size it -- what sizes it is how much of the host is left unclaimed.
#
# The cluster puts ONE executor on each host, and one of those hosts also carries the
# driver. So the budget that matters is that host's, and only its own executor is in it:
#
#   executor heap            16
#   executor JVM overhead     2
#   RMM pool 0.12 x 121      14.5   (unified memory -- the pool is host RAM)
#   pinned pool               2
#   driver heap               8
#   master + worker daemons   2
#   OS and page cache         6
#                          ------
#                            50.5   leaving ~70 GiB of 121 for the driver's tensors
#
# against 45 GiB for a leg with no edge features and ~55 GiB for one with them. An
# earlier draft of this file said 24g and 0.15, which left three gigabytes of margin --
# not margin, just a slower way to reach the same OOM.
#
# large-run.env spends 94 GiB of the same host on the executor side (48 heap + 42 pool
# + 4 pinned). Assembly cannot fit underneath that, which is the arithmetic behind the
# 2026-08-25 host OOM: the driver asked for memory the profile had already promised
# elsewhere, the box went to swap, and the kernel killed the executor -- the largest
# process, though not the one doing the asking.
#
# THE BIGGEST LEVER IS NOT IN THIS FILE. vector_dim drives the dominant term: at 256 the
# node features are 10.6 GiB instead of 42.4 and the leg fits with room to spare. It is
# an experiment variable, though, set per run in pyg_config -- not a sizing knob.

# 16g, not 48g: the assembly leg's Spark work is a sort and two joins over 76.7M rows of
# three integer columns, which is small next to what enrichment does. Cached frames use
# MEMORY_AND_DISK, so a tight executor spills rather than fails. Every gigabyte not spent
# here is a gigabyte the driver's tensors can use.
export EXECUTOR_MEMORY=16g

# Sizes the JVM only. The driver's real appetite is numpy, which this does not bound;
# 8g is comfortable for the Arrow batches and the plan bookkeeping of a ~40-stage job.
# Raising it takes memory away from the tensors it is meant to protect.
export DRIVER_MEMORY=8g

# On a unified-memory GPU the RMM pool is host RAM, so it competes with the driver
# directly. 0.12 caps it near 14.5 GiB -- ample for sorting 76.7M rows of three integer
# columns. Expect some "maximum pool size exceeded" retries; they are non-fatal, RAPIDS
# retries and spills. Slower GPU stages are the right trade for a driver that fits.
export RAPIDS_GPU_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.08
export RAPIDS_GPU_MAX_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.12

# Four task slots per executor, each with a quarter of the pool above.
export GPU_PER_TASK=0.25
export RAPIDS_PINNED_POOL=2G

# Same reasoning as large-run.env: a busy executor can go quiet longer than the stock
# 120s without being dead, and evicting one destroys checkpoint blocks that have no
# replica.
export NETWORK_TIMEOUT=600s
export EXECUTOR_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=30s

# Move collected columns as Arrow buffers instead of one object per row. Spark's default
# is off. This is the difference between ~20 bytes an edge and a few hundred, and it is
# what keeps the chunked edge collect inside its budget.
export PYSPARK_ARROW_ENABLED=true

# Spark's own default is 1g. The chunk budget in edge_mapper.py bounds one collect at
# roughly 400 MB, so this is headroom -- if a run approaches it, lower the budget rather
# than raise this.
export DRIVER_MAX_RESULT_SIZE=8g

# ALL renders a plan per execution and buries the log. Turn it on deliberately when
# diagnosing GPU fallback -- it is the only source for the REASON a fallback happened.
# With NONE, multi_experiment.ipynb reports GPU placement from the Spark event log, where
# every operator the plugin claimed is named Gpu*, rather than calling it "NO GPU".
export RAPIDS_EXPLAIN=NONE
